<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>In the first attempt, <m>f(n) = an^2 + bn + c</m> and <m>f(c) = c(ac + b + 1)</m>.</p>
<p>(a) Give an example of a quadratic <m>f(n)</m>, with whole-number coefficients, for which <m>f(c)</m> is prime. Then the attempt is wrong for that quadratic. Type, for example, <c>2*n^2 + 3*n + 5</c>.</p>
<div><m>f(n) =</m> <fillin characters="20" name="ans1"/>  </div>
<p>(b) The attempt claims that <m>c(ac + b + 1)</m> cannot be prime because it has the two factors <m>c</m> and <m>ac + b + 1</m>. In which of these cases can <m>c(ac + b + 1)</m> still be prime? Select all that apply.</p>
<div><ol marker="(1)">
<li><m>c = 0</m></li>
<li><m>c = 1</m></li>
<li><m>c = -1</m></li>
<li><m>c \geq 2</m> and <m>ac + b + 1 \geq 2</m></li>
<li><m>ac + b + 1 = 1</m></li>
</ol> </div>
</p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>A prime number is a whole number greater than 1 whose only positive divisors are 1 and itself. So a prime <m>p</m> does have two factors, 1 and <m>p</m>. The first attempt forgets this: <m>f(c) = c(ac + b + 1)</m> can still be prime when one of its factors is 1 or <m>-1</m>.</p>
<p>For example: <m>f(n) = n^2 + 1</m> has <m>c = 1</m> and <m>f(1) = 2</m>; <m>f(n) = n^2 - 3n + 3</m> has <m>ac + b + 1 = 1</m> and <m>f(3) = 3</m>; <m>f(n) = 4n^2 - 1</m> has <m>c = -1</m> and <m>f(-1) = 3</m>.</p>
<p>When <m>c = 0</m>, <m>f(c) = 0</m>, and when both factors are at least 2, <m>f(c)</m> has a factor other than 1 and itself: in those cases the attempt’s step is right. But a proof must work for every quadratic, so the attempt fails.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>n^2+1</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>(2), (3), (5)</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>